<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Commonwealth of Dominica</h1><p class="st-meta">1978 CE – present · capital Roseau</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Mountainous, forested island republic between Guadeloupe and Martinique, independent since 1978 and home to the Kalinago Territory.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-dominica">Colony of Dominica</a> became independent on 3 November 1978 as a republic within the Commonwealth, with a president elected by parliament and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patrick-john">Patrick John</a> as prime minister.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hurricane-david">Hurricane David</a> devastated the island in 1979, and John's government fell amid scandal. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eugenia-charles">Eugenia Charles</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominica-freedom-party">Dominica Freedom Party</a>, prime minister from 1980 to 1995, was the first woman to lead a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> government; she survived coup plots in 1981, one involving John and foreign <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>, and backed the US intervention in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/grenada">Grenada</a> in 1983. Bananas were the main export until European preferences ended. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinago-territory">Kalinago Territory</a> preserves the region's largest indigenous community.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roosevelt-skerrit">Roosevelt Skerrit</a> has been prime minister since 2004. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hurricane-maria">Hurricane Maria</a> caused enormous damage in 2017, and Dominica, which funds much of its development through citizenship by investment, aims to become a climate-resilient nation.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/commonwealth-of-dominica.jpg" alt="Commonwealth of Dominica" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Alwin Bully · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Dominica.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1978 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Roseau</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Patrick John, Eugenia Charles, Roosevelt Skerrit</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English and Dominican Kwéyòl</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/dominica">Dominica</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Dominica</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=2002&amp;focus=commonwealth-of-dominica" aria-label="Commonwealth of Dominica on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3004 1609 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Commonwealth of Dominica lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3324 1865L3324 1865"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3324 1865h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3324 1865h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3324 1865h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Roseau. <a href="/atlas?year=2002&amp;focus=commonwealth-of-dominica">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
